# C035 (Cloudflare terminology alignment — the Rosetta layer). A DOCUMENTATION-grade Step: it adds a
# vocabulary-equivalence mapping + one reserved name, and changes NO type, NO schema, NO normative section. It
# rides entirely on the shipped C027 composable-units glossary (Route/Skill/Agent/sub-agent) and the read-only
# Cloudflare Agents SDK docs (developers.cloudflare.com/agents, snapshot dated 2026-06, vendored locally as
# cloudflare-agents-llms-full.md). Provenance: operator asked to align Suluk on Cloudflare's terminology + frame
# the agent-pyramid (session 2026-06-23). Ceilings are documentation-grade (<=0.7) and never raise C027's own 0.52.

# THE ROSETTA MAPPING IS DOCUMENTED, NOT A RENAME. Each Suluk composable-unit noun maps to a Cloudflare noun AND
# to the artifact it projects to, WITHOUT renaming any field. CONTEXT.md's C027 glossary already chose its nouns
# deliberately (Route carries `_Avoid_: tool (ambiguous with MCP tool)`), so alignment = a legibility layer over a
# considered choice, never an overrule. Suluk Route == CF server-side Tool (projects to an MCP tool); Suluk Agent
# == CF Agent (contract vs runtime instance); Suluk sub-agent == CF sub-agent / agent-as-tool; x-suluk-approval ==
# CF needsApproval; thinking.maxRounds == AI-SDK stepCountIs(n); x-suluk-jobs == CF scheduled tasks.
claim cf_terms_rosetta_documented: Bool @>=0.7 = True
falsified_when cf_terms_rosetta_documented == False

# THE ONE GENUINE COLLISION (Skill) IS KEPT + RESERVED, NOT RENAMED. Suluk Skill = the agent's model+instruction
# tier (presence of `model` is the hard route-vs-skill discriminator, C027). Cloudflare Skill = loadable on-demand
# content (its `agents/skills` catalog + the loadable Session context block) that carries NO model. A Suluk skill
# CONFLATES two CF concepts: (a) the CF agent's model-binding (model/tier/modelProfile -> CF agent model config)
# and (b) CF read-only "soul"/system-prompt content (provenance.source -> the GENERATED SKILL.md, which is itself
# a valid CF Agent-Skill artifact). Resolution (operator-chosen 2026-06-23): KEEP `skills` unchanged (honors
# shipped, council-resolved, D1-gated C027); RESERVE the name `x-suluk-resources` for CF's loadable-catalog
# concept, to be defined ONLY when Suluk grows runtime/memory descriptors (built-by-nobody until then).
claim skill_collision_kept_and_reserved: Bool @>=0.7 = True
falsified_when skill_collision_kept_and_reserved == False

# THE PYRAMID IS THE EXISTING DETERMINISM GRADIENT, NOT A NEW CONSTRUCT. The operator's agent-pyramid (deterministic
# floor -> less-deterministic/more-convenient top) IS Suluk's route(no-model)/skill(model) discriminator made into
# layers: Layer 0 = routes (guarantee same-in-same-out) projecting to MCP tools (the "calculators"); Layer k = an
# agent = skills(model) + routes(layer<k) + sub-agents(layer<k); maxDepth bounds the height (a leaf = maxDepth 0).
# Edges = MCP connections (each agent projects to a @suluk/mcp server; routes/sub-agents are the MCP tools the
# parent consumes). An agent's LEVEL is a PURE STATIC derivation over the route/skill/sub-agent graph — DECLARED /
# derivable, never schema-enforced, never read by the D1 matcher. No new normative kind; nothing here touches ADA.
claim pyramid_is_route_skill_gradient: Bool @>=0.65 = True
falsified_when pyramid_is_route_skill_gradient == False

# PER-LAYER STATIC OBSERVABILITY REUSES SHIPPED INFRA. The operator's three asks already have homes, so the pyramid
# adds an observability VIEW, not new enforcement: hardening -> gradeAgent (A-F) + @suluk/harden.combineGrades;
# token-warning -> contextBudget / thinking.budget declared vs the @suluk/agents context analyzer estimate;
# context-waste -> tier (resident-surface size) + round-accretion in context.ts. The optional layerReport is a
# COMPOSITION of these, not a new mechanism.
claim pyramid_observability_reuses_shipped: Bool @>=0.65 = True
falsified_when pyramid_observability_reuses_shipped == False

# HONEST CAVEAT: two of the Cloudflare anchors are EXPLICITLY EXPERIMENTAL in the vendored docs — `agents/skills`
# ("Agent Skills are experimental, and script execution in particular is early") and the Session memory APIs
# (`agents/experimental/memory/session`, "import paths and details may change before graduation"). So the mapping
# rows that touch CF Skills + CF context-memory are PROVISIONAL and may drift; the Rosetta doc says so inline, and
# the alignment's ceiling is bounded accordingly (<=0.7). The stable rows (Agent, Tool, sub-agent, needsApproval,
# scheduled tasks, x402 paidTool) carry the equivalence; the experimental rows carry it with a drift flag.
claim cf_experimental_anchors_flagged: Bool @>=0.8 = True
falsified_when cf_experimental_anchors_flagged == False
